From 67f3100b92828aa6b367466c985a36a820145f1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: QtScript Qt 6 patch set <qtscript@example.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:49:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] Restore sloppy-mode globals and resource-based extension
 init scripts

Two fixes that together let compiled-in extension init scripts behave
like upstream QtScript:

- Under a custom activation scope, evaluations that performed a plain
  undeclared assignment (e.g. `baz = 456`) were hoisting the new global
  property into the activation object and deleting it from the runtime
  global. That inverted the classic sloppy-mode contract: an assignment
  to an undeclared identifier creates a global property. Leave such
  globals on the real global object; only declared names (var/function)
  are copied to the activation object via the existing declaration
  bindings. The QSet<QString> declaredNames bookkeeping accumulated
  during the declaration scan is no longer consulted once this hoisting
  block is gone; drop it along with its population to avoid leaving
  dead code behind.

- QScriptEngine::importExtension() scanned only library-path 'script'
  directories for the per-extension __init__.js. The QtScript extension
  mechanism also supports shipping the init script as a compiled-in Qt
  resource at ':/qtscriptextension/<prefix>/__init__.js', typically
  bundled with a static plugin. Without that path a static plugin's
  init script was never evaluated (only its C++ initialize() ran), so
  globals and <prefix> content set up by the script were absent. Add a
  resource lookup before the library-path fallback so static extensions
  behave as documented.

The two changes are coupled: extension init scripts set up their API
via undeclared assignments in sloppy mode, which must land on the real
global object to persist for callers of importExtension().
